<Project>

    <!-- Repo-wide package identity, shared by all four shipping projects. The four packages
         (SlideRule, .Analyzers, .Xunit, .Cli) version in lockstep: this
         <Version> is the only MSBuild-side declaration. It must agree with the two copies in
         .mcp/server.json (.version and .packages[0].version); release.yml refuses a tag unless all three
         equal the tag. The nuget metadata below (authors, licence, project/repo URLs, SourceLink
         knobs, symbol packages) is identical across the four, so it lives here rather than being
         copy-pasted into each csproj. The one exception is stated where it is made: the analyzer
         package ships no build output, so its own project file turns the symbol package off.

         Four packages, eight shipped assemblies. SlideRule.SpecAnalyzers and
         SlideRule.SpecCompletion do not pack: both go out inside SlideRule, under
         analyzers/dotnet/cs. SlideRule.Extraction and SlideRule.Roslyn do not pack either:
         they go out inside the tool and the xUnit adapter, and extraction inside the analyzer package too.
         All four take the identity pin below from here like every other project. -->
    <!-- The binding identity is pinned and deliberately does NOT track <Version>. AssemblyVersion is what
         a spec project's compiled reference to SlideRule carries, and the default binder rejects a
         host whose copy is older than that reference — so letting it track the package version meant every
         release minted a new identity and broke specs built against a newer contract than the tool running
         them. FileVersion and InformationalVersion still track <Version>, so "which build is
         this" stays answerable. Nothing binds on AssemblyVersion, and the one place that prints it is the
         message for a spec built against another contract; ServerVersion, the SARIF driver and the two
         analyzers' version check all read InformationalVersion. 1.0.0.0 is the value to pin to because it
         is what the SDK assigns a source-built or vendored contract, which puts packaged and source builds
         on one identity, and it outranks every version shipped before the pin (0.1.0.0, 0.2.0.0) so their
         specs still bind.

         The pin covers the two analyzer directories too. SlideRule and SlideRule.Analyzers
         each carry a copy of SlideRule.dll beside their analyzer, so a project referencing both
         hands the compiler two copies under one identity. At one version they are one file, which the
         analyzer package's shape check holds, and every compiler handles them: the .NET compiler loads each
         package's directory on its own, and a .NET Framework compiler server that meets new copies after
         an upgrade refuses that compilation and shuts down, so the build compiles once without it. At two
         versions no identity helps, since the SDK's package conflict resolution passes only the newer
         copy and a .NET Framework process holds just one, so one analyzer is handed the other version's
         contract. Each analyzer therefore compares its own version with the copy it was handed before it
         does anything else, and reports a difference as its fault warning, SR2093 or SR1093, instead of
         checking. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <Version>0.2.0</Version>
        <AssemblyVersion>1.0.0.0</AssemblyVersion>
        <!-- FileVersion defaults to AssemblyVersion, not to Version (Microsoft.NET.GenerateAssemblyInfo
             .targets), so pinning one would silently pin both. Restated from Version, with any prerelease
             suffix trimmed because a file version must be four numeric parts. -->
        <FileVersion>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Match($(Version), '^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+')).0</FileVersion>
        <Authors>Andrew Gray</Authors>
        <Copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 Andrew Gray</Copyright>
        <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
        <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/andypgray/sliderule</PackageProjectUrl>
        <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/andypgray/sliderule</RepositoryUrl>
        <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
        <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
        <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
        <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
        <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- Deliberately NO $(CI)-conditioned build policy here — not the lock-file policy
         (RestorePackagesWithLockFile / RestoreLockedMode) and not ContinuousIntegrationBuild.
         RestorePackagesWithLockFile and ContinuousIntegrationBuild live one level down in
         src/Directory.Build.props (plus an inline lock-file copy in the tests csproj);
         RestoreLockedMode lives in every tracked csproj, for a reason src/Directory.Build.props
         records — Scorecard's Pinned-Dependencies check cannot see an imported props file.

         Reason, shared by both: fixture solutions are staged under a project's bin/ and built there
         mid-test-run — FixtureRestorer shells `dotnet restore` on them, and MSBuildWorkspace runs
         design-time builds over them — all without stripping $(CI) from the child process. Those
         throwaway copies sit inside the repo tree, so they walk up and import whatever props file is
         above them, and this is that file. A repo-root policy therefore reaches code it was never
         written for:

         - A lock policy would demand committed lock files for the temp fixture projects and, in CI,
           fail their locked-mode restore outright, breaking the temp-copy contract.
         - ContinuousIntegrationBuild implies DeterministicSourcePaths, which requires SourceRoot
           items. A non-SDK-style project in the 2003 MSBuild XML namespace has no SourceLink and so
           no SourceRoot, and its design-time build fails with "SourceRoot items must include at least
           one top-level (not nested) item" — a workspace-load diagnostic that reds `check` on the CI
           legs only. That is a real bug this file shipped, found 2026-08-03 (see the ClassicApp
           fixture and ClassicProjectCheckTests).

         Scoping both below the repo root keeps the shipping projects deterministic and locked while
         leaving the fixtures with no lock file to be held to — which is also what makes the
         RestoreLockedMode each of them now declares inert. -->

</Project>
